The attic
=========

Source that is kept but is not part of the system.

cmd/ holds one directory per program that goes into /bin, and the
intent is that everything on the system has its source there.  That
only reads clearly if the things which are NOT on the system live
somewhere else.  This is somewhere else.

Nothing here is built.  It is not in any DIRS, no makefile above it
descends into it, and moving something in here is how a program stops
being part of the tree without being deleted.

What is in it, and why it is not in cmd:

    whitesmith  The Whitesmiths compiler - pp, p1, p2, asm, util and
                the rest.  Its object format and its calling
                convention are both documented in cmd/sh/NOTES and
                lib/objects, because reading its binaries is still
                how the reconstructions are done, but ccc is the
                compiler now.

    cc          The v7 cc driver, swizzled to run hitech's and
                whitesmith's passes - cp1, cp2, optim, link, with -H
                to pick hitech.  Both of those compilers have left the
                build; ccc is the driver.

    asz.old     The TRASM-derived assembler, replaced by cmd/asz.

    Make        The make micronix used, until the v7 one gave up the
                name to it.  It was also what the retired top-level
                Makefile bootstrapped, back when a system without a
                make had to climb out of the hole by itself; the cross
                compiler builds one now.
    v7make      The v7 make, which had the name first.
    make.d      A third make.
                cmd/make is the one that is built.

    ash         The v7 Bourne shell.
    msh         A small shell.
    osh         An older shell.
                cmd/sh is the shell, reconstructed from the 1.67
                binary - see cmd/sh/README.  These are here because
                they were read while working out what that one does,
                and a shell to compare against is worth keeping.

    yacc        yacc, and
    yacc.d      a second copy of it.

    nroff       a roff, in command.c / escape.c / macros.c, and
    croff       the v6 one, n1.c through n10.c with the hyphenation
                digram tables in hytab.c.
                Micronix does not format with either.  /bin/man execs
                form(1) and the pages call its primitive requests
                directly - there are no macros anywhere in the tree,
                and man/README says so at length.  cmd/form is the
                formatter this system has.

    ansi2kr     ansi2knr, dated 1996: converts ANSI C to K&R C.  A
                tool for porting things IN, not a program that belongs
                on a Z80 running a v6 kernel.

    pp.xx       a post-processor for a compiler this tree stopped
                using, which had not tracked anything in a long time.

Nothing above is dead in the sense of being wrong; it is dead in the
sense that the system does not contain it.  Anything here can be read,
and several of them are read regularly - the point of an attic is that
you can go up and look.

